> Do you have a concrete example where this would be an issue?

Sure, many authors will use stateset to reason about the state their in (rather than tracking booleans). Here's an in-the-wild example: https://github.com/quietui/quiet/blob/431f4cf8253bb672483659dcad05b49d00c2d91e/src/components/combobox/combobox.ts#L834

```js
    // If we're already focused and have items, ensure dropdown is open
    if (this.customStates.has('focused') && this.filteredItems.length > 0 && !this.open) {
      this.open = true;
    }
```

If we were able to do something like `el.attachCustomStates().add('focused')` this would influence this component in ways that the custom element author might not have anticipated.

> Why is it not a desirable property for CSS custom states?

Role might be contextual to the point where a custom element author might have difficulty ascertaining the role given the context the element is in. `attachInternals().role` is there more to supply a sensible default. This is not the case for custom states which are not very likely not a default but instead the element declaring a state it is in (for example `:state(loading)`, `:state(loaded)`). Of course, if a custom element author _wants_ to make custom states capable of override they can.
